      "title": "رأس (raʾs) — the head",
      "headword": "رأس",
      "romanization": "",
      "gloss": "the head — a Semitic root cognate with Hebrew rosh, and the source of \"president\" in Arabic itself",
      "script": "arabic",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:292a1ee7bb8af3e2",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more ancient Semitic root — and a word still hard at work in modern political vocabulary, the same way Latin's caput is in English."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know — The word",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "رأس (raʾs) = \"head\" — an ancient Semitic root, cousin of Hebrew ראש (rosh), the very word inside Rosh Hashanah, \"head of the year.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way — From \"head\" to \"president\"",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Arabic builds رئيس (raʾīs), \"president, chief, head [of an organization],\" directly on this same root — literally \"the one who is the head.\" That's exactly the move English makes with Latin caput: captain (\"head\" of a unit), capital (\"head\" city). Different language families, the same very human idea — the person or place \"at the head\" is the one in charge."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"raʾs\" — head",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"raʾs\" — head]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"raʾīs\" — president, literally \"the head [one]\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"raʾīs\" — president, literally \"the head [one]\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the Hebrew cousin — \"raʾs / rosh\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the Hebrew cousin — \"raʾs / rosh\"]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does رأس mean, and what's its Hebrew cousin? (\"Head\" — cousin of rosh, as in Rosh Hashanah.) What word is built on it for \"president,\" and what does it literally mean? (رئيس raʾīs, \"the head [one]\" — the same \"head = leader\" idea as English captain/capital from Latin caput.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
